<h2>Our Perception of Truth</h2>

<h3>Perception of Truth Through Belief</h3>
<p>Beliefs are shaped by emotion, culture, faith, and long-held assumptions, often accepted without proof.</p>
<p>Thinking is hard, so people tend to judge quickly instead of deeply questioning. As a result, beliefs are often not challenged—they are accepted as reality and carried forward without reflection.
</p>

<h3>Perception of Truth Through Thinking</h3>
<p>Thinking is an active process of reasoning, questioning, and evaluating evidence to form conclusions. It is flexible and can change with new information, but it can still be biased or incomplete.</p>

<h3>Why This Perception Happens</h3>
<p>What we think or believe is often shaped independently of actual truth because the mind relies on mental shortcuts to process information quickly.</p>
<p>Cognitive biases—like confirmation bias and anchoring—distort judgment, making us favor familiar or initial information.
</p>
<p>As a result, our sense of reality is often shaped more by habit and perception than by objective truth.</p>

<h3>Why Do We Have Different Perception About Truth and Reality</h3>
<p>Is it because we are stupid?</p>
<p>Is it because we have different information?</p>
<p>Is it because our environment shapes our own opinion and identity?</p>

<hr>

<h2>Truth and Reality</h2>

<h3>Subjective Truth and Reality</h3>
<h4>My Personal View on Morality</h4>
<div class="group">
  <p>I don’t personally believe in a god or a higher authority.</p>
  <p>I think that since there is no superior entity deciding what's fair or unfair, what's right or wrong, our sense of morality comes from human needs, experiences, and social agreements rather than any universal rule.</p>
  <p>So nothing is actually wrong; we act according to our need</p>
  <p>This makes morality largely subjective—shaped by culture, perspective, and time.</p>
  <p>Even our attempt to define morality as objective or subjective is itself subjective.</p>
</div>

<h4>Reality Through Our Senses</h4>
<div class="group">
  <p>Even our brain doesn’t portray reality exactly as it is. It does its best to interpret the world through our limited senses—like sight, hearing, smell, taste, and touch.</p>
  <p>Different animals experience reality in completely different ways. Some can see ultraviolet light, others can hear frequencies we can’t. What we see and hear is only a small slice of what actually exists.</p>
  <p>This means our understanding of reality is not the reality itself, but a simplified version shaped by our biology. We don’t perceive the world as it is—we perceive it as we are.</p>
</div>

<h4>Subjective Reality in Nature</h4>
<p>From the perspective of trees and plants, tigers are saviors and protectors, but from the perspective of deer, they are predators.</p>

<h4>Donquixote Doflamingo (One Piece): On Justice and Power</h4>
<p>Pirates are evil? The Marines are righteous? These terms have always changed throughout the course of history! Kids who have never seen peace and kids who have never seen war have different values! Those who stand at the top determine what's wrong and what's right! This very place is a neutral ground! Justice will prevail, you say? But of course it will! Whoever wins this war becomes justice!</p>

<h4>Marcus Aurelius (Meditations) on Opinion and Perspective</h4>
<p>Everything we hear is an opinion, not a fact. Everything we see is a perspective, not the truth.</p>

<h3>Actual Truth and Reality</h3>
<p>This exist independently of us—they do not depend on what we believe or think.</p>
<p>Truth remains true whether or not we fully understand it; even before Isaac Newton, gravity still existed.</p>
